{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said to Abram, etc.</b> The reason that HaShem spoke to him before appearing to him — which He did not do with any of the other created beings — there are two reasons for this. Either it may be because he, upon him be peace, exerted himself to know his Creator in a way that no other person did, and at the age of five he recognized his Creator on his own through his wisdom (Nedarim 32a) — therefore there was no need for a revelation to him; rather He spoke His words to him, and the Speaker was already recognized by him." "Or it is possible that since he was the first person in holiness — for in the ten generations that preceded him there was no person who recognized His divinity, and Elohim did not speak with him — therefore HaShem conducted Himself with him in this order: He did not show His Shekhinah to him until He tested him, to see whether he would fulfill His decrees. And after he fulfilled His words and went forth from his land, etc., then He revealed Himself to him, as it is written (Genesis 12:7): ״וַיֵּרָא ה׳ אֶל אַבְרָם״ — unlike the rest of the created beings, in whose hearts faith had already preceded and been implanted through Abraham and who were rooted in holiness, so to them He would reveal Himself first. Go out and learn this from the Patriarchs and from Moses at the bush. And this approach accords with the words of our Sages, of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 39), who applied the verse (Psalms 45:11) ״שִׁמְעִי בַת וּרְאִי״ etc. to Abraham our father: first ״שִׁמְעִי״ — שִׁמְעִי (Hear), and that is the verse's saying <b>And HaShem said to</b> etc.; and afterward He revealed Himself to him, ״וַיֵּרָא״ etc., which is the verse's saying ״וּרְאִי״ (and see). And these two — hearing and seeing — were not simultaneous but with an interval between them. Therefore Scripture did not say ״שִׁמְעִי וּרְאִי בַת״ or ״בַת שִׁמְעִי וּרְאִי״, but separated ״שִׁמְעִי״ from ״וּרְאִי״." "<b>Go for yourself from your land, etc.</b> After it says ״from your land,״ there is no place to say ״from your birthplace,״ for when he leaves his land, behold, he is also leaving his birthplace." "<b>However,</b> the reason is that in the scale of the pain of separation, the pain of separating from his land is less than the pain of separating from his birthplace, and the pain of separating from his birthplace is less than the pain of separating from his father's house. Therefore He arranged the gradations from lesser to greater, in order to give him reward for each and every particular, in the manner of the verse's saying (Genesis 22:2) ״אֶת בִּנְךָ״ etc., for HaShem seeks to bestow good, blessed be He, upon those who love Him." "It further intends to say that he should go forth from his land, but not in the manner of his departure from Ur Kasdim, where he took along with him his birthplace and his father's house; rather he alone should leave his land and separate even from his birthplace and even from his father's house. But Abraham did not understand this from HaShem's words, and he took Lot with him. Or it is possible that he did understand thus, but Lot clung to him, as the verse says (Genesis 12:4) ״וַיֵּלֶךְ אִתּוֹ לוֹט״ — he clung to him (the word אִתּוֹ implying attachment). And although it says afterward (Genesis 12:5) ״וַיִּקַּח אַבְרָם״ etc. ״וְאֶת לוֹט״ etc. — the meaning is that he did not push him away, until he could find a way that would not shame him. Therefore you find that when he found a minor pretext — that the shepherds multiplied — he immediately said to him (Genesis 13:9) ״הִפָּרֶד״ etc. ״אִם הַיָּמִין״ etc. And the matter is nearly astonishing, that he would say such things to him with so much distance [of departure]; rather, since he was devising plans to separate him in accordance with HaShem's word, therefore as soon as he found a pretext he pushed him away with both hands. And I have seen fit to strengthen this interpretation as well from what Scripture says (Genesis 13:14): ״וַה׳ אָמַר אֶל אַבְרָם אַחֲרֵי הִפָּרֶד לוֹט מֵעִמּוֹ שָׂא נָא עֵינֶיךָ״ — this utterance proves that HaShem was sitting and waiting for when Lot would be separated, in order to show him the land that He had promised him at the very outset of His word to him, ״אֲשֶׁר אַרְאֶךָּ״ — and He did not show it to him until now, when Lot was separated; and this is the meaning of the first utterance: ״לֶךְ לְךָ מֵאַרְצְךָ וּמִמּוֹלַדְתְּךָ וּמִבֵּית אָבִיךָ״ — then אַרְאֶךָּ (I will show you)." "<b>Further</b>, it may intend to say in this vein: <b>go for yourself</b> — for your own benefit. And although goodness is not contingent on human striving, as they said (Psalms 75:7) ״לֹא מִמּוֹצָא וּמִמַּעֲרָב וְגו׳ הָרִים״ and so forth, and the Sages of blessed memory expounded (Numbers Rabbah ch. 25) in this language: every instance of 'mountains' in Scripture is according to its plain meaning except this one, which is in the language of 'lifting up' — end of quotation. Nevertheless there is a reason in the matter on account of a change of place, and this is His saying <b>from your land</b>. And they of blessed memory said (Ḥullin 91b): three things — even though there is no snake, there is a sign — and they are: a house, an infant, and so forth. And they further said (Bava Metzi'a 75a): one who suffers in this town and does not go to another town cries out before HaShem and is not answered." "<b>And from your birthplace.</b> After the manner of what they of blessed memory said (Rambam, Laws of Character Traits, ch. 6): 'A person should always dwell in the neighborhood of a lion rather than in a city with the wicked' — end of quotation." "<b>To the land that I will show you.</b> The meaning is that He did not inform him, in order to see whether he would hasten to perform the decree of the supreme King without knowing the place — and this is one of the trials. Further, they hinted that He would show him all of it, as it is written (Genesis 13:14) ״שָׂא נָא עֵינֶיךָ וּרְאֵה צָפוֹנָה וָנֶגְבָּה״ and so forth, and He gave him the power of sight to see all of it." "<b>Further</b>, it may be explained after the manner of what they said in Sifrei on the verse (Numbers 35:34) ״אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי שׁוֹכֵן בְּתוֹכָהּ אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי שׁוֹכֵן בְּתוֹךְ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — the Shekhinah does not dwell except among the children of Israel and while they are in the land — end of quotation. And this is what HaShem said to Abraham, <b>to the land that I will show you</b> — meaning: it is fitting for you and you are fitting for it. And the measure of the phrase is: I will show you to it and I will show it to you, for one without the other they are not fit for the indwelling of the Shekhinah and for an abundance of blessings."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {5 "The passage from Bava Metzi'a 75a is rendered here as it appears in the Hebrew; the saying is actually from Bava Batra 12a in most editions, but the citation reads Bava Metzi'a."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:40:59.145279430Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}